<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tiryns</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.) · capital Tiryns</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mycenaean citadel near the Argolic Gulf whose Cyclopean walls, among the most massive of the Bronze Age, so impressed later Greeks that they credited them to giants.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tiryns stands on a low rock near the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>, settled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>, when a large circular building was erected on its summit. Mycenaean rulers built a fortified palace from c. 1600 BCE, and in the 14th and 13th centuries BCE the citadel was enclosed by walls up to 8 metres thick, with vaulted galleries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The palace had a megaron with painted floors and frescoes of women in procession and boar hunts. Tiryns probably served as the harbour and partner, or subordinate, of nearby <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mycenae">Mycenae</a>. A few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets and inscribed jars show palace administration. Greek myth tied it to Perseus and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, who served King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurystheus">Eurystheus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The palace was destroyed c. 1200 BCE, but unlike most centres Tiryns continued, with a large lower town and a rebuilt hall into the 12th century BCE. It later became a small city absorbed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> c. 470 BCE. Its walls are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> together with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mycenae">Mycenae</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region